I've ended up following, again, roughly the same&nbsp;
as it was published in 2010 in The 4-Hour Body.&nbsp;

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:33.480
This is a strength training and resistance&nbsp;
training program. It's a very minimalist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:39.800
but in the last four to six weeks I've&nbsp;
probably gained 10 to 15 pounds of muscle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:44.760
and lost a decent amount of fat in the process.&nbsp;
That's more diet-dependent than anything else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:49.400
the fat-loss side, and that would be slow-carb&nbsp;
diet. But when you're approaching say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:55.000
progressive resistance, you want&nbsp;
to follow the metrics very closely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.560
have some type of plan in advance so&nbsp;
one could argue that's optimizing.&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:05.240
But then there's deoptimizing, and if you look&nbsp;
at my kitchen table and the books strewn about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:11.160
including this one: Masterpieces of Fantasy&nbsp;
Art. This is a TASCHEN book, or a TASCHEN book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:21.040
Dian Hanson. This is a gift that was put in&nbsp;
front of me, to my glee, about two weeks ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:27.800
and I've been digesting that very slowly. As I&nbsp;
have been digesting different types of philosophy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:33.720
and poetry, which certainly I'm not trying to&nbsp;
speed read in any sense whatsoever and went for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:41.560
a long meandering, relatively unplanned hike.&nbsp;
That is, the path itself wasn't predetermined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:50.040
with my dog Molly earlier today. So those would&nbsp;
all be examples in my mind of unoptimized living.&nbsp;

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:57.040
