00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:48.920
So Fan Ho would be what comes to mind.
I'll do one more on the questions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:53.800
were pre-submitted from you all. This is from&nbsp;
Yavor. "Do you use personal coaching, and how?"&nbsp;

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:58.680
I have used a lot of personal coaching throughout&nbsp;
my life, certainly for sports, for dance, for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:04.840
let's just call it, executive coaching. I&nbsp;
would also check the box. I've worked with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:11.720
Jim Dethmer at conscious.is, that's the Conscious&nbsp;
Leadership Group, and I recommend their book The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:17.200
15 Commitments of Conscious Leadership, I believe&nbsp;
it is, which was initially recommended to me if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:25.240
I'm recalling correctly, Dustin Moskovitz.
And there are many others that I have used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:33.040
over time. So the short answer is yes. In terms of&nbsp;
how or why? I would say there are many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:41.280
breeds of coach. Some focus on offering strategic&nbsp;
advice. Others act as therapists in a sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:49.200
ask you a certain set of questions. They tend&nbsp;
to each have their toolkits and they elicit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:53.440
different types of thinking by asking questions&nbsp;
that you would not be inclined to ask yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:59.360
or perhaps respond to differently when you're in a&nbsp;
conversation. And then there are still others who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:05.840
simply hold you accountable. Or I should say not&nbsp;
so simply, in all cases, hold you accountable and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:12.400
they have other types of value that they offer.
But predominantly, say, for someone in my position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:20.080
right now in this chapter of my life as it's&nbsp;
unfolding, I think that the greatest benefit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:27.440
is having someone to stress test my narratives&nbsp;
and beliefs, which are sometimes so ingrained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:33.240
that you simply see that as reality. But to&nbsp;
have someone who can identify, perhaps, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:41.120
beliefs that you haven't stress-tested, to really&nbsp;
break assumptions that need breaking or rewrite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:46.240
narratives that need rewriting, number one.
Number two, to ask uncomfortable questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:51.000
Number three, to act as an accountability partner,&nbsp;
because if you're at the top of the pyramid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:55.360
organizationally speaking, or if you're&nbsp;
a solopreneur or anything like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:00.600
even if you're the CEO of a really large&nbsp;
company, if you're at the top and perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:05.320
you don't have as much accountability in certain&nbsp;
respects as you would like, I think a coach can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:11.040
add a lot of value in that capacity.
So those are three of the questions.&nbsp;

