00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:05.840
This is a question from Christina.&nbsp;
"Have you ever had imposter syndrome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:11.440
with a podcast guest? If yes, who and why?"
So I wouldn't say that I've had imposter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:18.880
syndrome. I would say that there are times when I&nbsp;
have felt very nervous and insecure and have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:25.360
very worried about stumbling and making mistakes.&nbsp;
I think there are a few guests who come to mind.&nbsp;

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:29.160
Certainly my first episode with Arnold&nbsp;
Schwarzenegger, that was 2015 and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:33.800
took a year, a year and a half to set up.&nbsp;
Jamie Foxx. Similar, that took a year and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:40.880
half or two years to set up. And I put incredible&nbsp;
extended preparation into both of those episodes.&nbsp;

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:48.760
To give an example that may not be as&nbsp;
obvious as an A-list celebrity. Ed Catmull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:56.520
who at the time I think was president of Pixar,&nbsp;
he may have held some other official title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.520 --> 00:15:06.120
but Ed was the first person I had on the podcast&nbsp;
who was both high profile and someone I had never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:13.680
spoken with before. So we had no preexisting&nbsp;
rapport, no preexisting friendship, and I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:19.560
incredibly nervous about that, which is evidenced.&nbsp;
We may have cleaned it up, but as he spoke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:26.760
I was so nervous every time he said something, I&nbsp;
did what, in Japanese, I guess would be aizuchi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:38.680
which is like a confirmation like "Hm, hm, hm."
So this "Mm, mm, mm," like someone who's hungry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:45.280
and looking at a plate full of delicious, hot&nbsp;
food drove people nuts. And it was a nervous tic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:49.800
which we almost certainly ended&nbsp;
up editing out significantly.&nbsp;

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:54.760
But I've never felt like an imposter, and I am&nbsp;
not sure why that is. I know imposter syndrome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.760 --> 00:16:01.680
and that term get used a lot. The idea that you're&nbsp;
going to be found out as a fraud or something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:05.720
that. But I've always tried to be very transparent&nbsp;
with my weaknesses and where I am in the learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:13.520
curve with everyone, including my audience. And&nbsp;
I think that prevents that or avoids it at least.&nbsp;

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:25.020
So if I am on the stage telling people that I&nbsp;
have A, B, and C weaknesses or that I lack D,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:31.360
E, and F experience, and sometimes I&nbsp;
say that explicitly in my conversations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:38.600
it's almost like preemptive body armor in a&nbsp;
sense. Not only against external criticism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:46.320
but self-criticism. I don't need any additional&nbsp;
fuel on the fire for negative self-talk. So that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:52.320
could be viewed as a way of bolstering my&nbsp;
confidence in whatever skills I might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:58.480
or in the skills that I'm developing by laying&nbsp;
it all on the table, if that makes any sense.&nbsp;

