Here's a question from David. This is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:48.680
language learning. "Tim, you've talked and written&nbsp;
so much about language learning, but not as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:52.720
about maintaining and revamping your languages.&nbsp;
I'm trilingual and working on language number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.720 --> 00:31:57.480
four, thanks in no small part to resources like&nbsp;
The 4-Hour Chef and feel that keeping up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:57.480 --> 00:32:02.520
all my languages is often just as much work as&nbsp;
acquiring them in the first place. What practices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:07.240
or processes, if any, do you have for keeping up&nbsp;
with your Japanese, Spanish, and other languages?"&nbsp;

00:32:07.240 --> 00:32:13.240
All right, I do have some approaches here.&nbsp;
So number one is something that's become much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.240 --> 00:32:20.120
easier in the last handful of years, and that&nbsp;
is movies, watch movies. Not only watch, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:20.120 --> 00:32:26.760
for instance movies in Japanese, so Japanese&nbsp;
movies with English subtitles. Watch English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:33.240
language movies with Japanese subtitles. And ditto&nbsp;
for Spanish, right? So watch English language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:37.440
movies you know well, ideally, right? It could&nbsp;
be something you've seen a hundred times like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:43.680
Die Hard or whatever, it doesn't really matter&nbsp;
with Spanish subtitles. And then conversely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:43.680 --> 00:32:52.280
watch Spanish language movies with English&nbsp;
subtitles. Preferably that's something that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.280 --> 00:32:57.360
say, a series or a television show. I just find&nbsp;
there's a little bit more grist for the mill. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.360 --> 00:33:06.480
you can get used to the speech patterns of various&nbsp;
protagonists and actors within a given series. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:12.720
I've done that with Spanish multiple times.
Another approach is linking them together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:19.040
So linking languages together. And what I mean&nbsp;
by that is if, for instance, you have learned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:19.040 --> 00:33:30.360
in my case, Japanese first, when I wanted to learn&nbsp;
German, I bought both Japanese language versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:35.920
of One Piece, which is a very famous manga,&nbsp;
very famous comic book. I bought German language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:35.920 --> 00:33:41.800
versions of the same One Piece comic book, which&nbsp;
is a global phenomenon. Many people know it now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:52.560
but certainly back in 2005 this would've been less&nbsp;
common in German and then later in Spanish so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:58.920
could read the same series. And if I'm reading&nbsp;
the German and I don't understand a section,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:58.920 --> 00:34:04.480
rather than going to an English-German&nbsp;
dictionary or a German-English dictionary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.480 --> 00:34:10.480
I'm going back to the Japanese and I'm using&nbsp;
the Japanese to learn the meaning of the German.&nbsp;

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:17.240
And by doing that, I'm reviewing my Japanese&nbsp;
while acquiring the German. And then later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:17.240 --> 00:34:25.760
for instance, when I did Spanish, I used the&nbsp;
German language version of One Piece as my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:25.760 --> 00:34:31.200
safety net for the Spanish language version of&nbsp;
One Piece. So I'm reading it in Spanish. If I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.200 --> 00:34:35.040
don't understand something, I'm not going to&nbsp;
English, I'm not going Japanese, I'm going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:40.600
my most recent language, which is going to be&nbsp;
in that case German, if that makes any sense.&nbsp;

00:34:40.600 --> 00:34:47.840
And what's beautiful about these comic books is&nbsp;
they are laid out page by page, pane by pane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.840 --> 00:34:57.040
typically in exactly the same way. So the dialogue&nbsp;
should map really cleanly. And another benefit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:57.040 --> 00:35:01.520
which I just alluded to for the comic books,&nbsp;
is that they are almost entirely dialogue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:09.840
Rather than exposition, dense prose that is&nbsp;
very literary in nature, almost all of it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:15.520
dialogue. And granted, they might be talking about&nbsp;
pirates or something else that's not going to fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:20.480
into your daily conversation all the time, but&nbsp;
a lot of it will and certainly the grammar will.&nbsp;

00:35:20.480 --> 00:35:26.080
All right, that is language learning.
Okay, this is a question from Krishna. Molly pup.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:26.080 --> 00:35:32.440
That's Molly pup right there, my dog. "Can you&nbsp;
please share more on your daily care and routine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.440 --> 00:35:36.240
for Molly? We have a puppy and want to incorporate&nbsp;
