on YouTube related to basics of dog training. I&nbsp;
am not the world's greatest dog trainer, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:48.920 --> 00:35:55.240
did spend a lot of time looking at different types&nbsp;
of training. As far as books go, I would strongly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.240 --> 00:36:02.160
recommend Don't Shoot the Dog by, I believe it's&nbsp;
Karen Pryor, who popularized clicker training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:10.080
using clicker training, which she had initially,&nbsp;
I believe, refined with aquatic mammals who really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:10.080 --> 00:36:14.880
don't respond to negative reinforcement, right? If&nbsp;
you're training a dolphin and they don't do what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:14.880 --> 00:36:19.520
you want them to do, you can't really fold up a&nbsp;
newspaper and smack the dolphin on the butt with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:26.360
"Bad dolphin." They just swim away. So you then&nbsp;
in turn get much better at positive reinforcement.&nbsp;

00:36:26.360 --> 00:36:30.920
This is also true if you're say, trying to train&nbsp;
a chicken, which is something I still want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:30.920 --> 00:36:36.520
learn how to do. I want to do a weekend course&nbsp;
in training chickens. And one of the quotes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:36.520 --> 00:36:40.280
the book I remember, I can't remember the&nbsp;
attribution, but it was, "No one should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:40.280 --> 00:36:46.120
allowed to have a child until they've been forced&nbsp;
to train a chicken." And there's a lot to it.&nbsp;

00:36:46.120 --> 00:36:50.360
So if you want to learn about conditioning&nbsp;
overall, and certainly as it relates to a dog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:50.360 --> 00:36:55.840
I think clicker training and Karen Pryor and&nbsp;
Don't Shoot the Dog are a great place to start.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:55.840 --> 00:37:01.320
There are some fantastic YouTube channels,&nbsp;
which I believe at least last I checked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:07.200
I referred to in the description and some of&nbsp;
my videos on the Tim Feriss YouTube channel.&nbsp;

00:37:07.200 --> 00:37:13.240
So you can take a look at those, but there are&nbsp;
various types or categories of training. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:19.480
safety, which should take precedence over, say,&nbsp;
kind of vanity tricks, right? Dog spinning around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:25.680
less important than the dog staying, say, in&nbsp;
the back of the car and not jumping out until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:29.960
you've issued a command that sort of releases&nbsp;
the dog to jump out because they could get hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:29.960 --> 00:37:36.440
by a passing car or something like that.
In terms of routines, lots and lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.440 --> 00:37:42.160
walking. Breed by breed different breeds will&nbsp;
have higher demands. If they're working dogs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:46.600
like a Border Collie, you really shouldn't&nbsp;
have that dog in an apartment in the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:50.880
for instance. They just require way too much&nbsp;
exercise for it to be compatible with most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:50.880 --> 00:37:57.600
people in a heavily urban environment. But big&nbsp;
dogs, really big dogs, and really little dogs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:57.600 --> 00:38:03.960
do pretty well in those constrained environments.
Molly is kind of like her old man, me, a former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:03.960 --> 00:38:10.080
athlete, not great endurance, very heat&nbsp;
intolerant. So her enthusiasm outstrips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:15.440
her capacity for work, which is great for&nbsp;
me because I can take her for an hour-long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:24.600
or two-hour-long walk and I can either do&nbsp;
that in the wilderness or do it leashed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:24.600 --> 00:38:30.720
usually attached around the waist and listen to an&nbsp;
audiobook if that's something that I want to do.&nbsp;

00:38:30.720 --> 00:38:36.200
But I just swam laps in the pool before jumping&nbsp;
onto this Q&amp;A in freezing cold water as a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:36.200 --> 00:38:41.360
to wake up. And that is one of her favorite&nbsp;
activities. And she'll run around the pool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:44.960
from end to end to end to end, basically&nbsp;
doing a shuttle run for the entire time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:44.960 --> 00:38:50.400
that I'm swimming. So a tired dog is a happy&nbsp;
dog. It's also true for humans, by the way. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:57.320
physically exhausted human is generally a happy&nbsp;
human, and a well-trained dog is a happy dog.&nbsp;

00:38:57.320 --> 00:39:04.840
Now, well-trained could be substituted with&nbsp;
a dog with clear rules is a happy dog. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:04.840 --> 00:39:10.880
I think that also applies to parenting&nbsp;
and kids, right? How consistent are you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.880 --> 00:39:16.320
how predictable are you and therefore perceived&nbsp;
as stable? I think all of these things apply.&nbsp;

00:39:16.320 --> 00:39:21.520
So those are a couple of recommendations, but&nbsp;
certainly when you look at Karen Pryor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:21.520 --> 00:39:25.360
clicker training, I also think if your puppy&nbsp;
is young enough, crate training is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:25.360 --> 00:39:31.280
greatest gifts you can give your dog. Do not give&nbsp;
your puppy the opportunity to make mistakes. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:31.280 --> 00:39:36.880
harder to remove behaviors than it is to prevent&nbsp;
them in the first place. So if you don't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:36.880 --> 00:39:43.440
your dog to chew on shoes, just get shoes out of&nbsp;
their range. Get them off of the floor so they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:43.440 --> 00:39:46.840
don't develop that habit and they get past&nbsp;
that teething phase. And then you'll never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.840 --> 00:39:52.600
have a problem with your dog chewing shoes.
Here's one. Mary: "Do you batch cook?" Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:52.600 --> 00:39:56.920
I do. Especially if I'm by myself, I'll batch&nbsp;
cook and then I'll eat meals for a while. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:56.920 --> 00:40:00.800
have been following the slow-carb diet, as&nbsp;
described in The 4-Hour Body. You can find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.800 --> 00:40:05.360
it online. If you just search "How to Lose&nbsp;
100 Pounds [on] the Slow-Carb Diet," there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:05.360 --> 00:40:11.720
a blog post that I wrote that lays out all the&nbsp;
basics, and you don't need the book for that.&nbsp;

00:40:11.720 --> 00:40:18.560
I've been cooking, for instance, chili. I have&nbsp;
chili in the refrigerator. Then, I cooked up some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:18.560 --> 00:40:28.640
95/5, so 95 percent ground venison, five percent&nbsp;
ground organs, Maui Nui Venison ground meat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:28.640 --> 00:40:33.960
which is the most nutritionally dense&nbsp;
meat you can buy in the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:33.960 --> 00:40:39.800
as far as I'm concerned. And they have the&nbsp;
analysis on the website to demonstrate just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:40.400 --> 00:40:47.240
how incredibly nutrient rich their meat is. And&nbsp;
their blends, especially with a bit of organ meat.&nbsp;

00:40:47.240 --> 00:40:53.480
I'll cook that. And the combination of that,&nbsp;
plus the chili, plus a little goat cheese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:53.480 --> 00:40:59.000
because you've got to have a little goat cheese,&nbsp;
plus some sauteed spinach, which was cooked at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:59.000 --> 00:41:06.600
the same time, that'll feed me for three or four&nbsp;
meals. And as someone who's currently flying solo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.600 --> 00:41:15.240
I'm about to leave the country, that is&nbsp;
a fantastic go-to meal that I can just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:15.240 --> 00:41:23.360
recycle over and over again. I'll generally, I&nbsp;
would say, cook, batch cook enough to feed me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:23.360 --> 00:41:30.720
for four to five meals. And then, I'll go out at&nbsp;
least once a day to have the social interaction.&nbsp;

00:41:30.720 --> 00:41:35.760
This is from Leif, L-E-I-F. "Are there&nbsp;
any global or national trends in the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:40.520
five to 10 years that aren't talked about&nbsp;
enough or you believe more people should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:40.520 --> 00:41:47.520
paying attention to? If applicable, how are you&nbsp;
personally preparing for this or these shifts?"&nbsp;

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:55.840
I would say one that comes to mind is, for lack&nbsp;
of a better term, digital emotional surrogacy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:55.840 --> 00:42:07.200
I'm sure there is a sexier or more elegant term&nbsp;
for this, but the inevitable development that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:07.200 --> 00:42:15.600
will have, I would say, within the next, probably&nbsp;
two years, photorealistic avatars that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:25.640
interact with through, say, virtual reality. And&nbsp;
if you haven't seen the demo of the Metaverse with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:25.640 --> 00:42:35.200
Zuckerberg with Lex Fridman on his recent podcast&nbsp;
on YouTube that showcases what this can look like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:35.200 --> 00:42:38.160
I would encourage everybody to at least&nbsp;
watch the first five minutes to get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:38.160 --> 00:42:43.840
taste of things to come with the ability&nbsp;
to interact with photorealistic avatars.&nbsp;

00:42:43.840 --> 00:42:47.800
Furthermore, with the ability to interact&nbsp;
with photorealistic avatars who might be your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:47.800 --> 00:42:54.800
favorite celebrity, like a Taylor Swift,&nbsp;
with very convincing facial expressions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.800 --> 00:42:59.480
we are getting to a point where companies&nbsp;
like Replika, for instance, Replika with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:59.480 --> 00:43:08.440
K at the end, K-A instead of C-A, where digital&nbsp;
companions are going to become, for many people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:08.440 --> 00:43:14.400
not just a supplement to human interaction,&nbsp;
but a replacement for human interaction.&nbsp;

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:24.600
I would say that the loneliness epidemic,&nbsp;
from my perspective, is probably only going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:24.600 --> 00:43:32.480
to get more nuanced, more complex and more&nbsp;
challenging to address, in some respects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:37.040
because especially for people like myself&nbsp;
who are introverts, I'm taking active steps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:37.040 --> 00:43:44.080
so I'll answer that second part of your question,&nbsp;
to maybe preemptively gird myself for this.&nbsp;

00:43:44.080 --> 00:43:48.240
But for those people who are already&nbsp;
intimidated or taxed by going out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:56.240
interacting with one person or groups of people,&nbsp;
you could see the case of, for many of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:56.240 --> 00:44:01.840
opting out completely. And I think we already&nbsp;
have problems with declining birth rates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:01.840 --> 00:44:08.040
and there are many countries that are below&nbsp;
replacement rates at this point. I am very curious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:08.040 --> 00:44:15.520
to see what societal impact that will have.
The way I am counteracting that for myself is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:15.520 --> 00:44:20.240
booking things on the calendar in advance.&nbsp;
And by in advance, I mean, at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:20.240 --> 00:44:26.600
I'm probably six months out booking trips, booking&nbsp;
time with friends, booking time with family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:26.600 --> 00:44:31.800
getting it on the calendar, putting money behind&nbsp;
it. It doesn't have to be a lot of money, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:31.800 --> 00:44:37.480
enough money that you can benefit from the sunk&nbsp;
cost fallacy and feel invested so you won't cancel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:37.480 --> 00:44:49.640
things. And really giving myself very few options&nbsp;
for opting out of social encounters I know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:49.640 --> 00:44:58.040
social interactions that I've proven to myself&nbsp;
over time are always in my best interest. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:58.040 --> 00:45:04.000
if I will drag my feet to get there in the&nbsp;
first place, I will leave being better off.&nbsp;

00:45:04.000 --> 00:45:09.040
Those are a few thoughts in terms of trends.&nbsp;
People are paying a lot of attention to, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:09.040 --> 00:45:16.680
AI, in broad strokes, or machine learning, in&nbsp;
broad strokes. But my interest, and certainly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:16.680 --> 00:45:23.920
what I'm also watching in my audience are some of&nbsp;
the societal implications and the psychological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:24.440 --> 00:45:32.720
mass psychological implications of these things.&nbsp;
You will be able to take steps to perhaps put a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:32.720 --> 00:45:37.440
moat around yourself to minimize the damage,&nbsp;
but this is something to pay attention to.&nbsp;

00:45:37.440 --> 00:45:43.840
And I would also say that, as these&nbsp;
tools become more and more convincing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:43.840 --> 00:45:51.200
we've blown away the Turing test, it's already&nbsp;
been beaten or passed, so as these tools and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:58.080
machines become more and more convincing, more&nbsp;
and more appealing, I think that there will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:58.080 --> 00:46:04.280
the very natural impulse to offload more and more&nbsp;
of the things that we currently handle in our own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:04.280 --> 00:46:12.120
heads or manually. And if you want to preserve&nbsp;
some of those abilities, you're going to have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:18.186
decide to be, perhaps, a selective Luddite, or at&nbsp;
least for periods of time, be a selective Luddite.&nbsp;

00:46:18.186 --> 00:46:24.000
For instance, how many people here would say their&nbsp;
parents are better at directions offline, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:24.000 --> 00:46:28.200
using Google Maps than the younger generations?&nbsp;
And I would imagine a lot of people would raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:28.200 --> 00:46:36.720
their hand. And this is perhaps not controversial&nbsp;
because people have decided to embrace something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:36.720 --> 00:46:44.600
like Google Maps or many other competitors to&nbsp;
help them with convenience and accuracy and so on.&nbsp;

00:46:44.600 --> 00:46:55.360
However, if you don't use it, you lose it,&nbsp;
and it's easy to embrace convenience and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:55.360 --> 00:47:03.880
recognize severe atrophy of capabilities&nbsp;
until it's very hard to reverse. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:03.880 --> 00:47:11.400
that that is a meta awareness that needs to&nbsp;
be developed as we are interacting with these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:11.400 --> 00:47:18.560
increasingly seductive and powerful tools. Long&nbsp;
answer, but these are things that I think about.&nbsp;

00:47:18.560 --> 00:47:24.760
All right. Here's a question from Andre. "I'll go&nbsp;
on a longer trip to Japan next year and I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

