This is a question from Andy. "Arthur C.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:23.760 --> 00:52:28.160
Brooks episode had an immediate impact upon my&nbsp;
life philosophies. Was curious whether it did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:28.160 --> 00:52:32.320
the same for you, if it had a big impact. You&nbsp;
two seemed to become fast friends. Thanks."&nbsp;

00:52:32.320 --> 00:52:44.360
Yes, the episode with Arthur was a real delight,&nbsp;
and I appreciated his ability to spin many plates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:44.360 --> 00:52:54.480
very well. He is, from a secular perspective,&nbsp;
very dedicated to studying the science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:54.480 --> 00:53:01.600
mostly on the social science side, but&nbsp;
certainly tries to tie that together with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:01.600 --> 00:53:09.000
neuroscience in the fields that he's exploring.
He is deeply religious, and I find that very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:09.000 --> 00:53:16.200
interesting. And I think the question of how&nbsp;
to create meaning more so than find meaning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:16.200 --> 00:53:23.080
but you could use both, how to create meaning&nbsp;
in a world of pessimism and nihilism where, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:23.080 --> 00:53:31.400
many countries, religion has fallen away, at least&nbsp;
in some of the more urban environments, is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:31.400 --> 00:53:37.760
to become an increasingly pressing question. I am&nbsp;
personally focused on that. For me, individually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:37.760 --> 00:53:46.200
but also as I just look at the different state&nbsp;
changes in various demographics in my audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:46.200 --> 00:53:50.640
it's very concerning. I do think about this a lot.
And I predicted, a few years ago, and we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:50.640 --> 00:53:55.400
already seeing this, but it will continue to&nbsp;
be the case and will accelerate dramatically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:55.400 --> 00:53:59.840
I think. There will be an explosion of what&nbsp;
you might consider new religions. Some of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:59.840 --> 00:54:08.520
will masquerade as other things, right? CrossFit,&nbsp;
veganism, whatever it might be, but very strongly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:08.520 --> 00:54:17.720
held belief systems that are defended with&nbsp;
religious fervor where people who are heretics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:17.720 --> 00:54:25.080
from within are certainly ostracized. These types&nbsp;
of groups, some of them with overt spiritual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:26.160 --> 00:54:35.080
overtones or laws/commandments/intentional&nbsp;
living rules, whatever you might call it.&nbsp;

00:54:35.880 --> 00:54:44.640
I think we will see a vast proliferation of&nbsp;
these things, as well as more uptake with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:44.640 --> 00:54:51.360
Judeo Christian or Abrahamic religions. I think&nbsp;
all of those things are going to become more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:51.360 --> 00:54:59.960
and more appealing to humans, who, love it or&nbsp;
hate it, seem to be inextricably combined with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:59.960 --> 00:55:09.280
religion or the seeking of this thing called God.
And even in the most secular of societies, people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:09.280 --> 00:55:16.400
worship something. And I think the people who&nbsp;
are at most risk of self-deception and societal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:16.400 --> 00:55:23.880
problems are those people who aren't clear on&nbsp;
what they're worshiping. And this is barring from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:23.880 --> 00:55:29.040
I think it might've been from Infinite Jest,&nbsp;
but everyone worships something. It's just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:29.040 --> 00:55:34.000
question of knowing what you're worshiping. That&nbsp;
has certainly become of greater interest to me.&nbsp;

00:55:34.000 --> 00:55:37.120
This is a question from Reese.&nbsp;
"Weightlifting for longevity. What are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:37.120 --> 00:55:44.200
your thoughts? What are you doing differently?"
I think, if you're going to choose one type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:44.200 --> 00:55:49.640
exercise and one type of exercise only, resistance&nbsp;
training, which can sometimes take the form,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:49.640 --> 00:55:54.160
often takes the form of weight training, would&nbsp;
be the best investment for longevity and health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:54.160 --> 00:56:02.400
span. Certainly, for combating age related&nbsp;
decline of muscle mass and so on and so forth.&nbsp;

00:56:02.400 --> 00:56:09.400
There were a lot of questions that have come&nbsp;
up in the pre-submitted questions around, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:09.400 --> 00:56:14.400
the slow-carb diet, weight loss, how do you&nbsp;
make weight loss sustainable? And a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:14.400 --> 00:56:20.040
people have the experience of losing weight, and&nbsp;
then, regaining that weight. For instance, I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:20.040 --> 00:56:26.400
helping my dad right now with the slow-carb diet.&nbsp;
In the last, I want to say three months, he's lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:26.400 --> 00:56:34.240
53 pounds, so he's doing really well. But those&nbsp;
gains, meaning those losses, are hard to hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:34.240 --> 00:56:43.120
onto unless you upgrade your underlying machinery.
And what I mean by that is increasing muscle mass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:43.120 --> 00:56:53.080
your mitochondrial engine, so to speak, such&nbsp;
that you have greater ability to dispose of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:53.080 --> 00:57:04.080
glucose. You have better ability, with this&nbsp;
enhanced machinery, to generate heat, to manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:04.080 --> 00:57:14.280
your insulin and glucose response to food. Weight&nbsp;
training, really, we could look at the mortality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:14.280 --> 00:57:23.880
rates associated with broken hips and so on in&nbsp;
elderly populations. There's so many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:23.880 --> 00:57:31.160
outputs from pulling the one lever of consistent&nbsp;
progressive resistance with, say, weight training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:31.160 --> 00:57:35.360
that for me, it's just a no-brainer.
Yes, there are other things that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:35.360 --> 00:57:43.200
do and should do, probably, like zone 2 training&nbsp;
as described by Peter Attia. But for me, based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:43.200 --> 00:57:48.520
all of the data, based on, certainly, surveying&nbsp;
my audience, seeing what works, what doesn't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:48.520 --> 00:57:55.920
who's able to keep weight off, who's able to&nbsp;
sustain their progress over time, the common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:55.920 --> 00:58:02.400
factor that I've identified is weight training.
Now, why do I say weight training? Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:02.400 --> 00:58:08.400
I said resistance training because you can use&nbsp;
something like gymnastic strength training, GST,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:08.400 --> 00:58:15.720
which is fantastic, but it is, in some cases, more&nbsp;
challenging to quantify the progress. Whereas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:15.720 --> 00:58:20.800
if you're lifting dumbbells, 10 pounds&nbsp;
is 10 pounds, 12 pounds is 12 pounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:20.800 --> 00:58:29.440
15 pounds is 15 pounds. And in such a case,&nbsp;
it's really basic and very straightforward to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:29.440 --> 00:58:33.960
track your progressive resistance where you are&nbsp;
increasing the amount of weight or the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:33.960 --> 00:58:41.560
of reps and increasing the productive stress&nbsp;
that you're applying to your system over time.&nbsp;

00:58:41.560 --> 00:58:49.160
And I should note that that is another reason, why&nbsp;
if you have proper instruction, very, very key,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:49.160 --> 00:58:56.000
underline, underline, if you have proper technical&nbsp;
instruction, that free weights are often superior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:56.000 --> 00:59:01.720
to machines. If you're in one place and you&nbsp;
always will have access to the same machines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:01.720 --> 00:59:09.240
feel free to use machines. But otherwise, in my&nbsp;
case, when I am traveling, a lot of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:09.240 --> 00:59:17.800
dumbbells are, for instance, barbells are easy&nbsp;
to use anywhere I find them, and a pound is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:17.800 --> 00:59:24.280
pound or a kilogram is a kilogram. That's a long&nbsp;
answer to a short question, but if I had to pick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:24.280 --> 00:59:32.200
one tool for longevity in the exercise bucket, not&nbsp;
on the diet side of things or in other categories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:32.200 --> 00:59:38.200
I would choose, for myself, weight training.
All right. This is a question from Natalie. "Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:38.200 --> 00:59:42.960
Tim. What have you learned from years of&nbsp;
interviewing around asking the right questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:42.960 --> 00:59:47.640
and enabling meaningful conversations?"
I would say I've learned a whole bunch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:47.640 --> 00:59:54.120
and I'll probably do a recap, if such a thing&nbsp;
is possible, around the 10-year anniversary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:54.120 --> 00:59:58.320
of the podcast because I feel like I've&nbsp;
learned a lot. In terms of asking questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:58.320 --> 01:00:04.680
I would say that an interview is very much like&nbsp;
a conversation. If you meet someone at a cocktail&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:04.680 --> 01:00:09.400
party and your first or second question is, "Tell&nbsp;
me about the most traumatic experience of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:09.400 --> 01:00:13.000
life," they're going to think you're a crazy&nbsp;
person because nobody communicates that way.&nbsp;

01:00:13.000 --> 01:00:21.160
And there is a rapport-building, feeling-out&nbsp;
process, so I plan for that and I often will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:21.160 --> 01:00:29.080
spend, say, 10 minutes, five to 10 minutes before&nbsp;
we record having a conversation so that the guest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:29.080 --> 01:00:35.680
can feel more at ease with me so that we can then&nbsp;
get into some other corners. I will also set the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:35.680 --> 01:00:42.160
expectation in advance that they have final cut,&nbsp;
they have final edit, they'll get a transcript,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:42.160 --> 01:00:46.160
they can remove anything they want to&nbsp;
remove. I will also look out for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:46.160 --> 01:00:54.200
and for that reason, we should really explore&nbsp;
the edges. We should really push and let it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:54.200 --> 01:01:01.720
fly because I can always cut things out&nbsp;
later, but we can't add the interesting or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:01.720 --> 01:01:11.160
the unexpected back in later. That effectively&nbsp;
buys both of us permission to go places that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:11.160 --> 01:01:15.200
might not be typical for a conversation.
I always tell them where I'm going to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:15.200 --> 01:01:20.760
so they build confidence in the beginning and no&nbsp;
one stumbles out of the gates. Also, I would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:20.760 --> 01:01:27.000
look for the side alleys. Realizing that my&nbsp;
podcast is not live, for instance, sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:33.320
the digressions don't work. Sometimes,&nbsp;
the exit ramps that I think could take us&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:33.320 --> 01:01:38.120
somewhere interesting don't work. But other times,&nbsp;
they really end up being incredibly important.&nbsp;

01:01:38.120 --> 01:01:42.920
And one example that hops to mind is&nbsp;
my conversation with Debbie Millman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:42.920 --> 01:01:48.240
my first interview of Debbie Millman. And some&nbsp;
of you will recognize that name also because she&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:48.240 --> 01:01:54.480
and I had a conversation around childhood abuse&nbsp;
and our respective healing journeys from early&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:54.480 --> 01:02:02.520
childhood abuse. And the reason I felt comfortable&nbsp;
going there with her personally, for my own story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:02.520 --> 01:02:10.000
was that, when I first interviewed her, the list&nbsp;
of questions, all the prep was intended to focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:10.000 --> 01:02:20.040
on her background in graphic design and her career&nbsp;
trajectory, but I had noticed that, whenever she&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:20.040 --> 01:02:27.960
was asked about her childhood or her parents, she&nbsp;
would generally answer in one or two very vague&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:27.960 --> 01:02:34.360
general statements and move on. And I thought that&nbsp;
was curious and I wanted to learn more about it.&nbsp;

01:02:34.360 --> 01:02:38.720
I didn't suspect what was coming, but I&nbsp;
felt like at least we could cover some new&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:38.720 --> 01:02:42.520
territory. And I asked her if she would&nbsp;
be comfortable, this is part of my five&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:42.520 --> 01:02:46.000
to 10 minutes before we hit record, with me&nbsp;
asking her about her childhood. And she said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:46.000 --> 01:02:51.520
"Well, maybe. We'll see. You can try. And then&nbsp;
I'll answer if I feel comfortable." I asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:51.520 --> 01:02:57.040
her about this, and this was pretty early in the&nbsp;
conversation, I would say in the first half hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:57.040 --> 01:03:03.360
and it ended up being the first time she had ever&nbsp;
spoken publicly about childhood sexual abuse and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:03.360 --> 01:03:09.400
this incredibly traumatic period in her life and&nbsp;
everything she'd done to try to recover from that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:09.400 --> 01:03:22.400
And that was the rest of the interview, right?
So I always try to take a shot at the odd question&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:22.400 --> 01:03:27.360
or topic, maybe it comes up in my research,&nbsp;
maybe it comes up in the conversation where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:27.360 --> 01:03:32.000
there's a throwaway comment that might not be&nbsp;
a throwaway comment. And then I ask, "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:32.000 --> 01:03:36.440
let's come back to that for a second. How did&nbsp;
that make you feel?" Some very basic follow-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:36.440 --> 01:03:44.000
questions often to the trick. "What did you learn&nbsp;
from that? Walk us through that moment. What was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:44.000 --> 01:03:50.520
your internal experience? What was your self-talk&nbsp;
when A happened? Or when you did B?" These very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:50.520 --> 01:03:58.080
basic follow-up questions that take you off&nbsp;
script often lead to the most fertile ground.&nbsp;

01:03:58.080 --> 01:04:03.200
And also in the first, I would say 10 minutes&nbsp;
or so, I try to ask questions that they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:03.200 --> 01:04:09.360
never been asked before. Simply to&nbsp;
show based on my research with, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:09.360 --> 01:04:14.240
some very arcane point in Wikipedia that links to&nbsp;
another thing that links to another reference that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:14.240 --> 01:04:21.160
links to another reference and I get a quote from,&nbsp;
I'm making this up, but the second boss they ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:21.160 --> 01:04:27.880
had when they were working at an ice cream shop,&nbsp;
and I asked them what their interaction was like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:27.880 --> 01:04:32.520
with so-and-so, or who was so-and-so and how did&nbsp;
they fit into your life? And they go, "Oh, wow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:32.520 --> 01:04:38.600
okay. You really do your homework." And that snaps&nbsp;
them out of any autopilot dream state that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:38.600 --> 01:04:43.560
might've accidentally slipped into or deliberately&nbsp;
slipped into. People who get interviewed a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:43.560 --> 01:04:48.560
have their 60-minute set just like professional&nbsp;
comedians because they learn what works. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:48.560 --> 01:04:54.240
don't blame them. I can do the same thing.
So those are a few thoughts on things I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:54.240 --> 01:04:58.909
learned about interviewing. I could go on and on&nbsp;
and on, but those are a handful that come to mind.&nbsp;

01:04:58.909 --> 01:05:03.200
All right. This is from Lexi.&nbsp;
"30 grams within 30 minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

