back right side, say, between L5, L5 where I have&nbsp;
some stenosis and nerve impingement because of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:02.000 --> 01:13:10.040
bulging disc. I have found focusing on internal&nbsp;
rotation and external rotation, very helpful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:10.040 --> 01:13:16.600
That's something that Shirley underscored for me.
Definitely focusing on terminal hip extension when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:16.600 --> 01:13:22.120
walking. So ensuring that I'm pushing off of my&nbsp;
toe, in other words, ensuring that I'm pushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:22.120 --> 01:13:31.280
off and getting that last bit of the stride as&nbsp;
opposed to pulling my leg forward with the hip&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:31.280 --> 01:13:36.800
flexor. Instead of pulling it forward, stop. Pull&nbsp;
it forward, stop. I'm trying to push off and make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:36.800 --> 01:13:45.840
sure I feel that in my toe on each foot and then&nbsp;
have that leg swing through. And on top of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:45.840 --> 01:13:50.800
there are any number of different things&nbsp;
I could add to this, of course. But foam&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:50.800 --> 01:13:58.640
rolling the piriformis and the IT bands and TFL,&nbsp;
I've found incredibly helpful. I use a Hyperice&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:58.640 --> 01:14:05.000
vibrating foam roller, which seems to relieve&nbsp;
spasms much more effectively than other things.&nbsp;

01:14:05.000 --> 01:14:11.440
And then avoiding certain things. So if I&nbsp;
sit on a very hard wooden chair, my QL, my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:11.440 --> 01:14:17.400
Quadratus Lumborum, and external obliques and so&nbsp;
on will spasm to try to support that area because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:17.400 --> 01:14:23.080
of the compression sensitivity. So I just have a&nbsp;
small pillow that I throw in my car. So if I go&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:23.080 --> 01:14:27.320
to a restaurant, I'll just bring that in. Sounds&nbsp;
ridiculous. But for right now, it really, really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:27.320 --> 01:14:34.480
helps. Also, having proper chair ergonomics set&nbsp;
up. So even at this kitchen table where I'm doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:34.480 --> 01:14:39.720
this recording right now, I'm sitting in an Aeron&nbsp;
Chair with lumbar support with some foot elevation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:39.720 --> 01:14:47.760
on the floor so that I have support instead of,&nbsp;
say, leaning forward on my elbows the whole time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:47.760 --> 01:14:52.800
and fidgeting back and forth in such a way that&nbsp;
causes the stabilizing muscles to light up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:52.800 --> 01:15:00.760
then ultimately spasm. Hopefully that is helpful.
And yes to everyone who's asking, I have read the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:00.760 --> 01:15:07.520
Sarno books, I'm going to reread them, but not&nbsp;
all back pain is emotional or psychological.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:07.520 --> 01:15:14.560
Some of it is, absolutely. I'm going to reread&nbsp;
these things. The last year of my life has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:14.560 --> 01:15:19.920
incredibly stressful from a relationship&nbsp;
perspective, especially having a five-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:19.920 --> 01:15:28.640
relationship end roughly one year ago. And all&nbsp;
of the shifting sands that have come about after&nbsp;&nbsp;

